issues.problem:
`.complete` holds only if the coordinator's sync(.ifDue) reaches the engine before the parked launch run has finished. That launch run starts when migration ends; inline executors and 4 KiB files make migration short on the resumed launch. If migration and the launch run finish first, sync(.ifDue) answers .notDue, which the coordinator publishes as .upToDate. MIGRATION.md §4 (:182) and K-7 already accept .upToDate for exactly this reason. Not proven, because nothing failed:
- PK3: 20 of 20 .complete;
- PK6: 30 of 30 calls started their own explicit run, even with an extra subscription hop;
- compatibility target ×20 at load 39-68: all passed;
- MigrationLaunchShimTests ×20 at load ~2 without TSan, and ×5 under TSan: all passed.

Nothing holds legacy migration in these two tests: the first test and launch 2 use the inline bulk executor. So `progress == .complete`, and the premise that the coordinator's sync overlaps migration, both depend on the sync reaching the engine before migration and the parked launch run finish. If the main actor stalls between start() returning and the sync, the launch run completes first. Then sync(.ifDue) answers .notDue, the coordinator reports .upToDate, and the test fails; the CCR-K1 path is never exercised either. I measured this with a scratch-only probe (not committed) that puts a wall-clock delay between start() and syncIfNeeded():
- first-launch shape: 10/10 .complete at 0–20 ms; 10/10 .upToDate at 50, 100 and 200 ms;
- resumed launch 2: 10/10 .complete up to 50 ms; 5/10 .upToDate at 100 ms; 7/10 at 200 ms;
- downloaded = 0 in every iteration, so R-STO-11 still holds.
It never failed in my stress runs: 30 sequential at load 15–333, 120 in 8 parallel workers, 16 TSan runs and 2 simulator runs. The first test's assertion dates from round 2/3; the resumed test's launch 2 is new in this close-out. The L5K-R2 table calls both post-conditions deterministic and does not list this case. It is L5-R14 pattern (a): nothing establishes the overlap the assertion needs.

The bullet is introduced as "the request as it was filed", but L5K-R4 lists "the CCR-K1 header" among the passages to rescope. As filed, it still:
- scopes the defect to "the first launch after adoption";
- describes the test whose download count sits in `withKnownIssue(…, isIntermittent: true)`, a wrapper since removed;
- ends with "Until then MIGRATION.md step 4 keeps the coordinator out of the launch task".
None of that is current any more (fixed in 9fe92fc and 3cd3768; step 4 now calls the launch sync safe). ADR-055's writer could copy it as current text.
